WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.689
Structural validation establishes that a
value

2
00:00:02.689 --> 00:00:05.061
has the expected arrangement and
representations.

3
00:00:05.061 --> 00:00:07.251
Semantic validation establishes that those
values

4
00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:09.623
make sense for the requested operation.

5
00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:13.089
Two individually valid dates can describe
a reversed interval.

6
00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:15.279
A positive integer can exceed the

7
00:00:15.279 --> 00:00:17.650
permitted quantity for a particular
account.

8
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.117
Two recognized options can be incompatible
when selected together.

9
00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:24.036
These are domain rules that require more
than

10
00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:26.773
successful parsing or a matching field
type.

11
00:00:26.773 --> 00:00:30.969
Keep the layers visible without forcing
every rule into one schema.

12
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:33.889
A local refinement can express a
relationship between

13
00:00:33.889 --> 00:00:36.625
fields when it needs no external context.

14
00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:40.457
Rules involving account settings, current
resource state, or other records

15
00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:44.653
usually belong in a service that has the
required trusted information.

16
00:00:44.653 --> 00:00:48.029
This separation also helps control
database work: reject invalid

17
00:00:48.029 --> 00:00:51.404
structure before issuing expensive
lookups, and avoid turning a

18
00:00:51.404 --> 00:00:54.871
reusable schema into a hidden collection
of remote operations.

19
00:00:54.871 --> 00:00:56.878
Authorization remains a distinct
responsibility.

20
00:00:56.878 --> 00:00:59.432
A project identifier can have perfect
syntax

21
00:00:59.432 --> 00:01:02.169
and still refer to another tenant's
project.

22
00:01:02.169 --> 00:01:05.088
A requested role can be a valid
enumeration

23
00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:08.555
member while the caller lacks permission
to assign it.

24
00:01:08.555 --> 00:01:11.109
Derive the actor and tenant context from

25
00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:13.846
the authenticated session or other
verified credentials.

26
00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:17.677
Enforce the relevant permission against
the actual resource and operation.

27
00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.323
A schema validates a submitted claim; it

28
00:01:20.323 --> 00:01:23.059
does not establish ownership or grant
authority.

29
00:01:23.059 --> 00:01:25.249
Persistence introduces another boundary
because trusted

30
00:01:25.249 --> 00:01:27.986
state can change between checking and
writing.

31
00:01:27.986 --> 00:01:30.540
An allowed state transition observed
earlier may

32
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.277
no longer be allowed at commit time.

33
00:01:33.277 --> 00:01:36.287
Preserve critical invariants with suitable
database constraints, conditional

34
00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:39.936
writes, or transactions, and handle a
rejected commit explicitly.

35
00:01:39.936 --> 00:01:43.403
The service should consume validated
input, obtain permitted context,

36
00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:46.961
apply domain rules, and commit only an
authorized transition.

37
00:01:46.961 --> 00:01:49.697
Keep failures controlled and stop further
effects.

38
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:52.617
This layered design makes it possible to
review

39
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:55.718
what the schema guarantees, what the
service establishes,

40
00:01:55.718 --> 00:01:58.820
and what storage must enforce under
concurrent requests.

